Native waveform tests
=====================

With a configured Wine build, run from this directory:

  make check WINE_BUILD_DIR=/path/to/wine-build BUILD_DIR=/tmp/winmm-tests

These tests include the production waveform.c with mocked platform services.
waveform-done checks unlocked worker callbacks, stopped and running writes,
failed Start, consecutive reroutes, format-query cleanup, and Reset racing
with Write or Restart. It also checks that Reset discards old-device playback
before new buffers are queued. No audio hardware or Wine runtime is used.

The frame-consuming mock also checks partially written headers and fully
written queues across reroutes, large clock positions, padding-query failure,
and drain/close/reopen cleanup. The Reset interleaving tests guard against the
earlier uncommitted detached-header design; they also pass on HEAD and do not
prove that the pre-existing playback/Reset race has been eliminated.

Live playback and reroute test
=============================

  python3 wave-play.py --runtime /path/to/private/wine --arch x86_64

Pass an installed runtime containing the changed winmm.dll, notification fixes,
and winepipewire.drv (the files directory for a Proton layout). The runner needs
Python 3, MinGW, PipeWire tools, WirePlumber with its policy profile, and
dbus-run-session. It creates a private server, session manager, prefix and two
virtual sinks; no desktop default or audio hardware is used. Use --work for a
new output directory and --arch i386 to test a private 32-bit build.

The probe plays silence through the real devices thread, checks completion
order/flags, resets mid-stream, changes the default during playback, and plays
again. Rerouting must create a replacement Wine stream actively linked to the
second sink before playback finishes, and Wine must log reroute_mapper_device.
Graph snapshots and Wine/probe logs are retained. Unlike the native fixture,
this catches a devices-thread call that stops completing headers.

Notification unload regression test
===================================

Run against a private installed Wine runtime containing the winmm and mmdevapi
changes and winepipewire.drv. The test needs Python 3, a MinGW compiler,
PipeWire, pw-cli and pw-metadata. It builds its own PE probe, creates a new Wine
prefix and starts a private PipeWire server with two virtual outputs. Desktop
audio and existing prefixes are not touched.

From this directory:

  python3 notification-unload.py --runtime /path/to/private/wine --arch x86_64
  python3 notification-unload.py --runtime /path/to/private/wine --arch i386

Use --work /path/to/new/directory to retain logs at a chosen location, --cc to
select the MinGW compiler, or --cycles to change the default 200 stress cycles.
Wine loaders and Unix libraries must belong to this private runtime, rather
than being symlinks which select a different installation.

The focused check holds the loader lock on the application thread, changes
the default output and waits for winmm's device mapping to follow it. Updating
that mapping requires winmm's notification callback to get past its TRACE.
Tracing is enabled deliberately: without the debug-helper initialization
before registration, the callback's first string formatting can wait for the
loader lock. The check fails with that initialization removed. It releases
the lock even on failure so the callback can finish before DLL cleanup.

The following unload/reload cycles are supplementary stress coverage. They do
not force each unload to overlap an active callback. This test does not prove
that arbitrary application callbacks can safely block or unregister clients.
The runner fails on a nonzero exit or a timeout; logs remain in its printed
output directory. The probe opens no audio stream and does not test WOM_DONE.
